1. Prometheus + PromQL Setup
Prometheus scrapes metrics from targets (your mining nodes, sensors, etc.) and stores them as time-series data. PromQL is the query language you use to pull that data.
Key PromQL examples for your dashboard:
# Sensor readings over last 5m
avg_over_time(sensor_temperature[5m])

# Alert rate
rate(alerts_total[1m])

# Node health percentage
(count(up == 1) / count(up)) * 100

# Displacement rate (your mining use-case)
deriv(sensor_displacement_mm[10m])
Where Prometheus runs:
- On Render: Deploy Prometheus as a Docker service on Render. It needs persistent disk for TSDB storage. Expose port 9090.
- Better option: Run Prometheus on a VPS/EC2 (Render's persistent disks are expensive for metrics). Then your Render web app just queries it remotely.
2. Backend Bridge (Your API Layer)
Your React frontend cannot query Prometheus directly from the browser (CORS + network isolation). You need a backend API:
Browser/App → Your API Server → Prometheus (port 9090)
Backend options (pick one):
- Node.js/Express — use prom-client library to query PromQL
- Go — use api/v1/query HTTP endpoint natively
- Python/FastAPI — use requests to hit Prometheus HTTP API
The Prometheus HTTP API:
GET http://<prometheus-host>:9090/api/v1/query?query=up
GET http://<prometheus-host>:9090/api/v1/query_range?query=rate(alerts_total[1m])&start=...&end=...&step=60
Your backend wraps these, adds auth, and serves JSON to your frontend.
